# /etc/hosts — the names of the machines in the room.
#
#   address   name [alias ...]        '#' starts a comment
#
# This file is older than DNS. It is SRI-NIC's HOSTS.TXT (1974), the one
# list every ARPANET host fetched nightly until the list outgrew the method
# and Mockapetris wrote DNS (1983) to replace it. It never went away,
# because for the three machines in the room it is still the right answer:
# no server to ask, no packet to wait for, no way to be wrong about where
# the build PC is.
#
# WHICH FILE WINS — the usual ladder, but MERGED rather than first-hit
# (Chris's ruling, 2026-08-22): /home/hosts is read first and /etc/hosts
# second, so YOUR names (the build PC's, on your own partition, surviving
# every refresh) sit on top of the system's, and a name in both is yours.
# Put the machine you os64get from in /home/hosts:
#
#   192.168.137.1   yogi          # the ICS host — the valet lives here
#
# Names here are case-insensitive, as DNS names are. Anything that is not
# found here is asked of the name server (see /etc/net.conf).
